From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J host: the client, in the player's process

What libbluray in the player does once a BLURAY plays in the host (bdr_client.c):

- Calls are forwarded and wait for their reply. HDMV overlays the host drew during a call are
  delivered by the caller, in order, before the call returns: the player's callback runs inside
  the call, as when libbluray plays locally.
- A receiver thread reads the connection and never calls the player: replies go to their
  caller, the host's file requests to a file thread, overlays to a callback thread. A player
  callback that blocks can not hold up the disc reads the host is waiting for.
- The host reads the disc only through the player: files and directories of the BD-ROM itself
  (disc_open_bdrom_path(), new; no overlay, no decryption), read-only, at most 256 open. A path
  must be relative, with no empty, "." or ".." component, no backslash and no control character.
  For a mounted disc or a folder, read with the system's functions, which follow symbolic links,
  what the path names must still be inside the disc root (realpath()); a link out is refused and
  said in the log.
- ARGB planes (PG, IG, BG) are drawn by the host into a memfd the player creates, sized for
  1920x1080 planes and sealed (no shrink, no grow) before it is passed, mapped read-only here.
  The receiver copies the rectangle of an update out of it, within the plane, and then
  acknowledges it, so that the host can draw the next one. The callback thread keeps the planes
  as the player sees them and gives the player the rectangle inside its plane, as libbluray does,
  and delivers what came before the end (the CLOSE of a plane when the disc is closed).
- An HDMV overlay is checked (plane, command, rectangle within 1920x1080, sizes of palette and
  RLE) and its RLE is made to give exactly w x h pixels before a player sees it: a run that goes
  past the end is cut, a picture that ends short is made up with transparent pixels. A player
  that decodes the runs until w x h pixels (Kodi) can not be made to write past its buffer.
- Event, read and clip replies are checked: no more data than asked, events of bluray.h, a clip
  name of five digits, whose CLPI is then parsed from the player's own disc.
- The host's log lines go to the player's log, without control characters, at most 100 a
  second (it says when it leaves some out, and how many).
- A host that breaks the protocol, does not answer a call for 60 s, or is closed with the disc is
  killed: by a pidfd opened from the credentials the kernel attaches to the host's hello (with
  SO_PASSCRED; on a socket systemd accepted, SO_PEERCRED names systemd, not the host). The hello
  comes before the host has read anything of the disc. A host running as root is refused. Once
  it is gone, bd_get_event() and bd_read_ext() give one BD_EVENT_ERROR (BD_ERROR_BDJ), and the
  calls answer as for a disc that can not play.

Tests only (-Dhades_bdj_test=true, never in an image): the client may start the host program
itself on a socket pair (LIBBLURAY_BDJ_HOST_EXEC) and shorten the timeout.
